---
// Lesson 6.5 — Platforming Studio: The Gauntlet
// Studio day. The cave scene ships EMPTY of gameplay: no Coin, no Spikes, no
// Turret instances anywhere in it, and the Spikes prefab has no collider - the
// student places and builds every hazard in this lesson. There is no boss door
// in this project and none is referenced. No UI: feedback is Debug.Log.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>6.5 — Platforming Studio: The Gauntlet</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Build day. Every skill from this unit — the flick, the honest ground check, the mercies,
    the counter — goes into one room that a stranger must finish without a single word from
    you. The grade is not difficulty and it is not art. The grade is <em>clarity</em>: a
    player who has never seen your cave should know where to go next by looking at it.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Studio Brief</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Everything in this room is yours to place.</strong> Read that again, because it
        is the thing people get wrong on build day: the cave scene ships with a floor and
        nothing else. There are no Coins in it, no Spikes in it, no Turrets in it. If your
        gauntlet needs a hazard, you drag it in from <code>Assets/Prefabs</code> and make it
        work — which means giving the Spikes a collider, the way you did in
        <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>, and giving anything you want to pick up a collider and a
        tag the way you did in <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> and <a href="6.4.html">6.4</a>.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Section</th><th>What it tests</th><th>What you must place yourself</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>1 — The gap</strong></td>
            <td>A jump across a hole with a running start.</td>
            <td>Two platforms with a gap between them. Extend the floor by painting on the <code>Collision</code> tilemap, or drop in sprites with colliders — either is fine.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>2 — The climb</strong></td>
            <td>A jump up onto a ledge, then another.</td>
            <td>Raised surfaces. Measure the height against what your <a href="6.1.html">6.1</a> numbers can actually reach — not against what looks right.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>3 — The spike run</strong></td>
            <td>Precision under threat.</td>
            <td>Spikes from <code>Assets/Prefabs/Spikes</code>, each with a solid collider you add, plus small platforms to cross them.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>4 — The soft gate</strong></td>
            <td>A promise: something visible you cannot have yet.</td>
            <td>A <code>Coin</code> from <code>Assets/Prefabs</code>, placed out of reach of one jump and reachable with two.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>5 — The gauntlet's end</strong></td>
            <td>A threat you pass rather than fight, and a clear finish.</td>
            <td>A ranged threat and a finish trigger. Use the turret from <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a> if yours is working; an enemy or a second spike run is a legitimate substitute.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the finish line.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>The finish is a trigger, so which object needs the collider with <em>Is Trigger</em> ticked — the flag, or the player? What does <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> say about the minimum for an overlap to be reported at all?</li>
            <li>Which callback fires, and what parameter type does it hand you? Which tag check tells you the thing that arrived was the player and not a stray projectile?</li>
            <li>What does it print? Your HUD lives in <code>Start</code> and reads your stats — it is not in this scene, and it does not know a finish line exists. So the Console is still how a tester learns they finished.</li>
            <li>Does the finish fire once, or every frame the player stands in it? How do you make it fire exactly once?</li>
            <li>Should the finish do anything else — stop the player, print a time, count attempts? Decide, and be able to say why you stopped where you did.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: dying has to mean something.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>What does your death rule from <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> actually do — reload a scene, or reset and move the player? Read your own code and state it in one sentence.</li>
            <li>If you reload, the player reappears at wherever the Player instance <em>sits in the saved scene</em>. Is that the start of your gauntlet? If you dragged the Player to the start during Play mode, was that position saved when you stopped?</li>
            <li>Where is your start pad — an actual object the player stands on, or just a remembered position? What happens to a tester's first thirty seconds if the room starts them mid-section?</li>
            <li>If your death rule loads a scene <em>by name or index</em>, read Build Settings before you answer: which scene is index 0, and does it contain a Player? Find out rather than assuming.</li>
            <li>What survives a death in your build — the coin count, the boots, the health — and what is lost? State it, then test it, then decide whether you accept the rule.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the camera.</strong>
        Main Camera carries a follow script from <code>Assets/Scripts/Package Scripts</code>
        with two Inspector fields: an offset and a checkbox. One of those fields decides whether
        the camera follows the player <em>vertically</em>. Section 2 is a climb. Decide what the
        camera should do during that climb, change the setting if you need to, and be ready to
        justify it — a room you cannot see is not a room you designed.
    </div>

    <p><strong>Rules of the studio:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>You may not explain the room verbally. Ever. The room teaches by layout or it does not teach.</li>
        <li>Every section must be passable two ways, or with two different timing windows. A pixel-perfect single solution is a bug, not a challenge.</li>
        <li>Keep a notes file open the whole time. Every "hmm" from a tester is a bug you have not built yet.</li>
        <li>Build order that works: floor and walls first, then the start pad, then one section at a time — test each one alone before you chain them — and tune the <a href="6.3.html">6.3</a> feel numbers <em>last</em>. Teams that tune feel first spend the afternoon tuning air.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Level design has a literature, and none of it is about video games. Read these for the
        principle, then name the section of your gauntlet each one is complaining about.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — A theme park queue (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The rope maze does not stop anyone. It just makes the wrong path
// obviously longer than the right one.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — A furniture showroom (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Every room is visible from the doorway. Nobody is ever told where to go.
// They are simply able to see it.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — A bowling lane (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The gutter is visible from the foul line, not discovered at the pins.
// A hazard you only meet at full speed is a hazard you cannot learn.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 4 — A building's fire escape (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Two ways down from every landing, because on the day it matters
// one of them will be blocked.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 5 — An attempt counter at a climbing wall:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>if (testerFell) attempts++; // the number that turns "hard" into "learning"</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Reading a Room</h3>
        <ol>
<li>The rope maze makes the wrong way <em>slower</em> without blocking it. Which section of your gauntlet does that, and which one just blocks?</li>
<li>The showroom assumes the visitor can see the whole space. Can a tester see your section 4 gate from the moment they could possibly need it — or only after they have already walked past?</li>
<li>The bowling alley puts the gutter where you can see it from the start. Where is your spike run visible from, and what speed is a tester moving when they first see it?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Playtest: Silent, With Numbers</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        The silent-watching rules from <a href="2.4.html">2.4</a> still apply — no hints, no
        pointing, no explaining. Today you also record <strong>numbers</strong> per section.
        Vague feedback is what you get when you did not write anything down.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Section</th><th>Deaths</th><th>Time (seconds)</th><th>What the tester said</th></tr>
        <tr><td>1 — the gap</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>2 — the climb</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>3 — the spike run</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>4 — the soft gate</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>5 — the end</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
    </table>



    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Ship a five-section gauntlet that a stranger finishes without instructions</li>
            <li>Say which unit skill each section tests, and point at the geometry that tests it</li>
            <li>Place and build every hazard yourself — spikes with colliders, coins with tags, platforms with surfaces</li>
            <li>State what your death rule does, where it puts the player, and what survives it</li>
            <li>Collect death and time data instead of vibes, and make one numbered change per round</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/index.html">Unit 6: Jumping</a> — everything this gauntlet tests</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/triggers-vs-collisions.html">Triggers vs Collisions</a> — the finish-line trigger, in full</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — colliders, tags and the callbacks you used today</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="7.1.html">7.1 — Tilemaps: The Level Editor</a> — Unit 7 begins, and the rooms you build get much bigger.</p>
</div>`;
